   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to the taxation of agricultural and horticultural lands in the town of Hempstead, Nassau county",
    "basePrintNo": "S3201",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3201-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §483, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3201\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the real property tax law, in relation to the taxation of\nagricultural and horticultural lands in the town of Hempstead, Nassau\ncounty\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo allow agricultural lands with an acre of land or greater\nto claim a property tax exemption in the Town of Hempstead.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nAmends the real property tax law so that \"lands\ndevoted to agricultural or horticultural use\" is defined as lands\ngreater than an acre in the town of Hempstead to allow tax exemption\nfor capital improvements.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nMost agricultural lands in the Town of Hempstead\noperate on extremely small budgets and have not been improved in\nsometime. As the law currently stands, five acres of land is required\nto qualify for a tax exemption for capital improvements. Given the\nhigh density of the Town Of Hempstead, the agricultural and\nhorticultural lands that are currently in operation have extremely\nlow acreage, lower than what is required. If the RPTL is not amended\nto allow tax exemptions for capital improvement, the agricultural\nactivity would cease, taking away an important part of Long Island\nhistory. without this amendment agricultural businesses on Long\nIsland would be doomed.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nS.5412 - Referred to Local Government\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nThere would be minimal fiscal implications for\nthe state of New York.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
