   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to exempting from taxation structures and buildings essential to the operation of a commercial equine operation in certain towns; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon the expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S3201",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3201-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3201A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nin relation to exempting from taxation structures and buildings\nessential to the operation of a commercial equine operation in certain\ntowns; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon the\nexpiration thereof\n\nPURPOSE:\nIn relation to exempting from taxation structures and buildings\nessential to the operation of a commercial equine operation in\ncertain towns; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nthe expiration thereof.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nAmends the real property tax law so that structures and buildings\nessential and actively devoted to the operation of a commercial\nequine operation during a specified period may be granted tax exempt\nstatus at local option.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nMost agricultural lands in the Town of Hempstead operate on extremely\nsmall budgets and have not been improved in sometime. As the law\ncurrently stands, five acres of land is required to qualify for a tax\nexemption for capital improvements. Given the high density of the\nTown Of Hempstead, the agricultural and horticultural lands that are\ncurrently in operation have extremely low acreage, lower than what is\nrequired. If the RPTL is not amended to allow tax exemptions for\ncapital improvement, the agricultural activity would cease, taking\naway an important part of Long Island history. Without this amendment\nagricultural businesses on Long Island would be doomed.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nS.5412 - Referred to Local Government\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nThere would be minimal fiscal implications for the state of New York.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed January 1, 2026.\n\n\n",
